  <section id="shading-01" class="field field-ink">
    <p class="word" aria-hidden="true">SHADES</p>
    <div class="copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Motorized Shading</p>
      <h1>Daylight, shaped around the room.</h1>
      <p class="deck">Quiet motorized shades manage glare, privacy, heat, and atmosphere while preserving the architecture of the window.</p>
      <a class="action" href="#shading-15">Plan Your Shading</a>
    </div>
    <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-01-room" subject="Glass-wall or bedroom shading photograph" ratio="16 / 9" />
  </section>

  <section id="shading-02" class="field field-paper">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>What must each shade accomplish?</h2>
      <p>Motorized shading is an architectural system, not an accessory selected after the room is complete. Fabric, openness, motor, power, pocket, fascia, bracket, control, and window geometry have to work together to manage daylight without compromising the intended interior.</p>
      <p>DAVG coordinates those details with the lighting and control architecture so shades can operate individually, by room, on schedule, or as part of a scene.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-03" class="field field-ink">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Light, privacy and the view</h2>
      <p>Morning light can enter gradually. West-facing glass can respond before glare overtakes the room. A cinema or media scene can lower the appropriate shades with the lights. Bedrooms can gain privacy without adding another visible device or daily task.</p>
      <p>When shading is right, the movement is quiet, aligned, and expected. The window remains the focus.</p>
    </div>
    <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-03-rooms" subject="Room examples of daylight, privacy, and view" ratio="16 / 9" />
  </section>

  <section id="shading-04" class="field field-paper">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2>Fabric, hardware, power and control</h2>
        <p>The design combines window dimensions, orientation, solar exposure, fabric performance, roll direction, hembar alignment, motor selection, power, structural blocking, pockets or fascia, control groups, keypad functions, sensors, and automation logic.</p>
        <p>Large or repeated openings require elevation-level coordination so adjacent shades align visually and move in a deliberate sequence.</p>
      </div>
      <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-04-system" subject="Shading system diagram" ratio="4 / 3" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-05" class="field field-ink">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Solar geometry and glare study</h2>
      <p>Fabric openness, orientation, pocket design, and shade control can moderate glare and material exposure while preserving the view. A shade does not automatically reject a fixed share of UV and heat, improve HVAC efficiency by a fixed amount, or require one pocket depth. Those results depend on the specified fabric, glazing, solar conditions, and construction detail.</p>
    </div>
    <form class="solar">
      <fieldset>
        <legend>Time of day</legend>
        <input type="radio" name="solar-time" id="solar-morning" checked />
        <label for="solar-morning">Morning</label>
        <input type="radio" name="solar-time" id="solar-midday" />
        <label for="solar-midday">Midday</label>
        <input type="radio" name="solar-time" id="solar-afternoon" />
        <label for="solar-afternoon">Late Afternoon</label>
        <input type="radio" name="solar-time" id="solar-evening" />
        <label for="solar-evening">Evening</label>
      </fieldset>
      <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-05-study" subject="Double-height glazing elevation with seasonal sun paths" ratio="16 / 9" />
      <p class="solar-note" data-time="morning">Morning light can enter gradually. Design the response around the sun and the room, then specify the fabric.</p>
      <p class="solar-note" data-time="midday">High sun changes which glass needs coverage. Design the response around the sun and the room, then specify the fabric.</p>
      <p class="solar-note" data-time="afternoon">West-facing glass can respond before glare overtakes the room. Design the response around the sun and the room, then specify the fabric.</p>
      <p class="solar-note" data-time="evening">Privacy after dark is a separate fabric decision from daytime glare. Design the response around the sun and the room, then specify the fabric.</p>
    </form>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-06" class="field field-paper">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Choose fabric, form and Lutron family</h2>
      <p>The order matters: goal at each window, then fabric and privacy, treatment form, visible or concealed design, shade family and feasible dimensions, power and control, then construction and investment.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What must the window do?</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Priority</th><th>Best starting point</th><th>Important limitation</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Keep a view and reduce daytime glare</td>
            <td>Solar-screen roller fabric sampled against that orientation; consider 1%, 3%, 5% or 10% openness</td>
            <td>More openness usually preserves more view but admits more direct light. An open-weave fabric is not dependable nighttime privacy when the room is brighter than outdoors.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Daytime glow with more privacy</td>
            <td>Translucent/privacy fabric</td>
            <td>It diffuses light but sacrifices the clear view. Inspect it from outside after dark.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Bedroom darkness</td>
            <td>Opaque/blackout fabric, with side channels, bottom/sill and pocket detail if strong light exclusion matters</td>
            <td>Blackout fabric alone does not eliminate edge gaps. Distinguish room-darkening from light-tight construction.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Both daytime view and night privacy/darkness</td>
            <td>Two separately controlled layers, when there is adequate mounting space, or a solar roller paired with drapery</td>
            <td>Dual fabric requires more depth, more drives and controls, and a careful sequence. Palladiom and Triathlon do not have the same dual-bracket options as Sivoia.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Large view wall or specialty opening</td>
            <td>Elevation-level shade schedule and engineered drive/fabric review</td>
            <td>Width, drop, aspect ratio, roll diameter, seam, support and access may force multiple shades or another treatment.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Lutron’s 1%, 3%, 5% and 10% openness options, and the distinction between translucent and blackout fabric, are a selection vocabulary, not a performance promise for an unspecified fabric. Sources: <a href="https://www.lutron.com/us/en/articles/how-to-layer-window-treatments-with-motorized-shades">Lutron fabric layering</a>, <a href="https://www.lutron.com/us/en/window-treatments/shades/roller-shades">roller shade types</a>, and the <a href="https://assets.lutron.com/a/documents/3686848_luxury_resi_shade_matrix.pdf">room-darkening design matrix</a>.</p>

    <h3>Which treatment belongs in the room?</h3>
    <p>Roller shades are the primary architectural path for broad glazing, a crisp hem and a restrained sightline. Honeycomb/cellular can suit a smaller window or a different softness or insulation brief. Roman shades introduce folds and a textile presence. Wood and horizontal-sheer blinds provide tilt or a different privacy and view behavior. Drapery is appropriate when softness, acoustic character or a larger fabric composition is the goal. These are not interchangeable versions of a roller. A complete style gallery stays unpublished until DAVG confirms which families it will sell and support. Lutron’s <a href="https://www.lutron.com/us/en/window-treatments/residential-shades">residential window-treatment overview</a> documents the available families.</p>

    <h3>Good, better, and best fit</h3>
    <p>These labels are editorial shorthand for fit and architectural ambition. They are not a manufacturer quality grade or an installed price.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Relative path</th><th>Likely fit</th><th>What changes beyond price</th><th>Power and design caveat</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Good — professionally specified Triathlon / Triathlon Select</td>
            <td>Finished rooms and several coordinated windows where minimal construction and a clean roller result matter most</td>
            <td>Battery or supported low-voltage variants, accessible fascia or open-roll choices, programmed scenes and retrofit flexibility</td>
            <td>Select is a narrower configuration. It is not a drop-in replacement for a concealed dual roller.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Better — Sivoia QS, including the correctly sized roller chassis</td>
            <td>New build or renovation where hidden pockets, large windows, specialty mounting, precise alignment or a dual layer drive the design</td>
            <td>Wider architectural configuration range. Roller 64, 100, 150, 300 or a specialty product follows the measured opening.</td>
            <td>Plan power, support, pocket and service access while construction is open. A wireless control link can still have wired power.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Best for visible craft — Palladiom wired or Wire-Free</td>
            <td>The shade mechanism is intentionally visible and must coordinate with architecture, finishes and Lutron wall controls</td>
            <td>Designed exposed brackets, matched bottom rail and finish choices. The wire-free version can achieve this look in a finished home.</td>
            <td>Palladiom is not automatically the right choice for a disappearing recessed pocket.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A concealed Sivoia roller may be the stronger decision for a top-tier residence. A Palladiom Wire-Free shade may be the stronger retrofit. Sources: <a href="https://www.lutron.com/us/en/window-treatments/residential-shades">Triathlon, Sivoia and Palladiom</a> and the <a href="https://assets.lutron.com/a/documents/3686848_luxury_resi_shade_matrix.pdf">technology and compatibility matrix</a>.</p>

    <h3>Shade-path comparison</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Shade path</th><th>Strongest fit</th><th>Architectural effect</th><th>Power and service</th><th>Primary tradeoff</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Triathlon / Triathlon Select</td>
            <td>Finished homes and straightforward roller conditions</td>
            <td>Low-profile visible top treatment or accessible open roll</td>
            <td>Confirm the model’s battery or supported wired-power option and future access</td>
            <td>Limited architectural configuration compared with engineered Sivoia pockets</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Sivoia QS concealed roller</td>
            <td>New construction and renovation with coordinated pockets</td>
            <td>The shade can disappear into the architecture when raised</td>
            <td>Confirm chassis, pocket, blocking, power and control route, service access and light gaps</td>
            <td>Integration depends on early coordination and the correct enclosure size</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Palladiom exposed roller, wired</td>
            <td>High-design openings where visible hardware is desirable and wiring is available</td>
            <td>Refined exposed bracket and bottom rail become part of the elevation</td>
            <td>Coordinate support, finish, alignment, power route and access</td>
            <td>Visible geometry and attachment must be resolved precisely</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Palladiom Wire-Free exposed roller</td>
            <td>Finished rooms needing the Palladiom exposed expression with less wiring work</td>
            <td>Similar crafted visual intent without a concealed power route</td>
            <td>Battery replacement access, RF design and exact size and fabric eligibility still matter</td>
            <td>Less construction does not remove the need for blocking and alignment</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Dual Sivoia roller, or a layered roller plus drapery</td>
            <td>Openings needing both view-through solar control and room darkening or privacy</td>
            <td>Two layers solve different daylight states</td>
            <td>Larger assembly, separate drives and more control and trim coordination</td>
            <td>Greater flexibility at higher spatial and installation complexity</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Exposed versus concealed, battery versus hardwired, and wired versus wireless communication are three separate decisions. Sivoia QS is not universally wired-communication only, and Palladiom is not invariably line-powered.</p>

    <h3>How large a shade can be chosen?</h3>
    <p>These are illustrative published configurations. They do not describe every Lutron shade or guarantee a quote. The selected fabric, drive, width and drop, mounting condition and current Lutron design tool have to approve the opening. DAVG checks the current model page and project sizing before release.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Published configuration</th><th>Example manufacturer limit</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Triathlon Select roller</td>
            <td>Up to 108 in wide × 120 in high, subject to its available configuration</td>
            <td>A useful finished-room reference. Oversize glass or a different top treatment may require another family.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Triathlon WIDR roller</td>
            <td>Up to 144 × 144 in in Lutron’s luxury residential matrix</td>
            <td>Width can be larger than standard Select. Fabric, weight and construction still determine the approved size.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Palladiom Wire-Free roller</td>
            <td>21–144 in wide × 12–144 in high, varying by fabric</td>
            <td>Large exposed openings can be viable without a concealed power run, if bracket support and battery access are engineered.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Sivoia QS Roller 100, a specific recessed 5-in configuration</td>
            <td>A listed 24–176 in single-panel width, varying by fabric and height, with a typical maximum area of 100 sq ft</td>
            <td>Maximum width and maximum drop are not promised together. Lutron sizes by configuration, area, fabric and aspect ratio.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Mounting, pocket and light gaps</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Inside mount</strong> preserves surrounding architecture but loses some usable fabric width to brackets and operator clearance.</li>
      <li><strong>Outside mount</strong> can overlap trim to improve coverage. The shade and brackets become visible and may interfere with doors, handles or drapery.</li>
      <li><strong>Exposed roller</strong> is an intentional design object. It does not require a ceiling pocket, and it still requires structural support.</li>
      <li><strong>Fascia</strong> conceals the roller below an existing ceiling. Evaluate its visible height, service access and proportion.</li>
      <li><strong>Recessed pocket</strong> must be coordinated for chassis, one or two rollers, roll diameter, hanger orientation, depth and height, blocking, wire entry and removable service access before framing. Lutron measuring application note 598 gives, for example, Roller 64 a 3.5 × 3.5 in minimum mounting space for a single shade and 5.5 in height × 7.5 in depth for a dual mount. Those are configuration-specific minimums, not a universal finished pocket. Roller 100, 150, 300 and special conditions can require more room.</li>
      <li><strong>Room darkening:</strong> a 0%-openness fabric can still allow light at the side, top or bottom. Specify the side channel, bottom bar or sill, and pocket condition when darkness matters.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Power, communications and everyday control</h3>
    <p>Wire-free, wireless, and battery are not synonyms. Ask separately how the motor receives power, how it receives commands, and how the owner acts. A wired-power Sivoia QS may communicate through wired QS or a supported wireless receiver. Palladiom is available wired and Wire-Free. Triathlon includes battery and supported low-voltage variants. The everyday result is a wall button or supported keypad scene, a scheduled position, app access when appropriate, and an immediate manual override. Shade control may share an intentional lighting keypad. There is no special wall slider or extra shade keypad required by default.</p>

    <div class="study-split">
      <div class="study-sticky">
        <p>View or blackout?</p>
        <p>Hidden or exposed?</p>
        <p>Battery or wired?</p>
      </div>
