    <h3>Fabric decisions that change the result</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Decision</th><th>What the homeowner experiences</th><th>What must be tested or documented</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Openness factor</td>
            <td>Balance among view-through, glare reduction, daytime privacy, and solar control</td>
            <td>Evaluate from the actual orientation and interior, not a loose sample alone</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Color</td>
            <td>Changes view, reflected light, exterior appearance, and perceived contrast</td>
            <td>Review against glass, wall, trim, and daylight conditions</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Blackout construction</td>
            <td>Controls direct light but depends on side channels, sill, pocket, and room conditions for complete darkness</td>
            <td>Define whether the goal is room darkening or near-total light control</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Single versus dual fabric</td>
            <td>One condition versus a flexible sheer and blackout combination</td>
            <td>Confirm pocket depth, roll diameter, bracket, and control grouping</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Railroaded or seamed fabric</td>
            <td>Enables certain sizes but changes visual continuity</td>
            <td>Approve seam placement and appearance before fabrication</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>What DAVG measures before specifying shades</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Finished and rough opening dimensions at multiple points.</li>
      <li>Window orientation, seasonal solar path, view priority, and nighttime privacy.</li>
      <li>Mullions, handles, trim, drapery, furniture, and door-operation conflicts.</li>
      <li>Pocket width and depth, blocking, power route, roll direction, fascia or reveal, and service access.</li>
      <li>Fabric roll diameter and alignment across adjacent openings.</li>
      <li>Grouping, speed and sequence, local control, scenes, schedules, and sensor logic.</li>
      <li>Integration with <a href="/solutions/architectural-lighting/">architectural lighting scenes</a> and the <a href="/solutions/home-intelligence/">whole-home control architecture</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Late shading decisions can reduce glass coverage, force visible fascia, expose wire, misalign adjacent shades, remove the dual-fabric option, or require a pocket that competes with structure and ceiling details.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-07" class="field field-ink">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Four opening studies</h2>
      <p>These are example briefs, not a specification or a claim that a named product works in every opening.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Opening and priority</th><th>Likely starting path</th><th>What to review</th><th>What can change the path</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>West-facing living-room glass; preserve a mountain view</td>
            <td>Solar-screen roller selected for glare and view</td>
            <td>The actual view and screen glare at the intended hour, plus fabric width, mullions and the exposed or recessed head</td>
            <td>Night privacy is a separate need. Add a second treatment or change fabric if the room must screen occupants after dark.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Street-facing bedroom; privacy at night and reduced dawn light</td>
            <td>Translucent or room-darkening treatment with an appropriate overlap, side channel or dual layer</td>
            <td>The exterior sightline after dark, and the side, top and bottom gaps</td>
            <td>Blackout fabric alone does not make a dark room. Egress, window operation and buildable side channels matter.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Double-height or unusually wide glazing</td>
            <td>A measured Sivoia chassis or another approved engineered configuration, with possible mullion-aligned divisions</td>
            <td>Every panel width and drop, seams, drive location, bracket structure and lift alignment</td>
            <td>Fabric weight, roll diameter, aspect ratio and available structure may rule out a single panel.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Finished sitting room with intentional exposed hardware</td>
            <td>Palladiom Wire-Free, or wired Palladiom if the size, support and finish fit</td>
            <td>Bracket and bottom rail, plus the installed elevation</td>
            <td>Battery access, trim, structure and a preference against visible hardware can favor Triathlon, fascia or a pocket instead.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
