  <section id="shading-08" class="field field-paper">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2>Pocket, clearance, blocking and power</h2>
        <p>The concealed work includes load-bearing blocking, pocket dimensions, wire paths, transformer and panel locations, accessible service conditions, fabric roll calculations, motor capacity, alignment tolerances, group addressing, and limit programming.</p>
        <p class="measure">Roller 64 example from Lutron note 598: 3.5 × 3.5 in minimum mounting space for one shade. A listed dual mount needs 5.5 in height × 7.5 in depth. Confirm the selected assembly before framing.</p>
      </div>
      <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-08-pocket" subject="Pocket, fascia, or exposed hardware detail" ratio="4 / 3" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-09" class="field field-ink">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>New build or finished-home installation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>New construction:</strong> confirm pockets, structure, power, and window dimensions before framing and drywall.</li>
        <li><strong>Renovation:</strong> coordinate with trim, ceiling, glazing, and electrical work while cavities are accessible.</li>
        <li><strong>Retrofit:</strong> measure finished conditions and choose between battery, targeted wiring, fascia, or exposed architectural hardware.</li>
        <li><strong>Replacement:</strong> document existing pockets, brackets, power, controls, and fabric widths before assuming new equipment will fit.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Four questions belong in the proposal. What framing, finish or electrical work is required per opening? Who provides bracket blocking, power and the final pocket dimensions? Can an installer remove a shade for service without damaging the finish? When will the opening be field measured, and who signs off on fabric, seam and hembar alignment? Neither a retrofit nor a new-build pocket has a universal price ranking.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-10" class="field field-paper">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Prevent fit and mounting problems</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No solid mounting structure:</strong> issue blocking details before walls close.</li>
        <li><strong>Pocket sized by guess:</strong> size from the selected shade, width, fabric roll, and configuration.</li>
        <li><strong>Misaligned hems across glass:</strong> survey and group the elevation as one composition.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrong fabric for the objective:</strong> evaluate view-through, glare, privacy, and darkening separately.</li>
        <li><strong>Visible wiring or inaccessible power:</strong> coordinate routes and service access early.</li>
        <li><strong>Automation that surprises occupants:</strong> make schedules legible and manual override immediate.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-12" class="field field-ink">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>What changes shade investment?</h2>
      <p>Important variables include shade width and height, opening count, fabric, single versus dual rollers, exposed versus concealed hardware, hardwired versus battery power, structural access, control integration, lift alignment, and custom finish requirements.</p>
      <p>Quote from a measured window schedule and confirmed architectural details. This page does not publish generic per-window pricing.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A straightforward battery roller at an accessible finished window usually needs less invasive construction.</li>
        <li>A wired multi-opening project adds power and coordination.</li>
        <li>Concealed large-format or dual-roller work adds pocket, structure, finishing and alignment.</li>
        <li>Exposed Palladiom may add finish and mounting detail without a pocket.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The owner should receive a line-by-line opening schedule showing drive, treatment, fabric, control, power, mounting, finish, anticipated light gap, service method and construction allowance. Installed budget bands stay unpublished until estimating records support them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-13" class="field field-paper">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Measure, commission and maintain</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Survey the glass and orientation.</li>
        <li>Define performance and privacy objectives.</li>
        <li>Select and sample fabric.</li>
        <li>Engineer pockets, structure, power, and controls.</li>
        <li>Field-verify dimensions.</li>
        <li>Install, align, set limits, program scenes, and teach local override.</li>
        <li>Preserve the final shade schedule and service-access notes with the home’s documentation.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>DAVG is a Lutron Certified HomeWorks Integrator. That work includes verifying platform, shade and control compatibility; measuring each opening; presenting fabric and hardware samples; creating the shade, power, control and pocket schedules; coordinating the builder, electrician and window or trim trades; ordering the approved configuration; programming grouping, scenes and limits; demonstrating owner and manual control; and documenting maintenance and battery or service access. Lutron is selected when its verified shade families, architectural hardware, control ecosystem and service path meet the brief.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="shading-14" class="field field-ink">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Denver sun and homeowner questions</h2>
      <p>At elevation, strong solar exposure and large temperature swings make west- and south-facing glazing especially demanding. A Denver-area shade strategy should account for seasonal sun angle, mountain-view preservation, privacy after dark, and the way high-contrast daylight affects screens and interior materials.</p>
      <p>Local shading decisions start with window orientation, privacy, view, pocket, and fabric. Cherry Hills Village, Greenwood Village, Castle Pines, Castle Rock, and west- and south-facing Denver homes are where those conditions most often define the brief.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faqs">
      <details>
        <summary>What's the structural requirement for mounting shades on a large window wall?</summary>
        <p>The bracket, fastener and substrate must support the selected shade and its operating load. DAVG details blocking or an approved structural attachment with the architect or installer, particularly where finished drywall will conceal the support. A measured opening and the specific installation instructions control the answer.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How much pocket depth do motorized shades actually need?</summary>
        <p>There is no universal depth. For illustration, Lutron’s measuring guide identifies 3.5 × 3.5 inches as minimum mounting space for one Roller 64 assembly and 7.5 inches of depth for a particular dual Roller 64 assembly. That does not determine the finished pocket for another roller, fabric, fascia or service detail. Size the selected assembly on a section before framing.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Are Lutron shades battery-powered or hardwired, and which is better?</summary>
        <p>Both exist. Battery models can reduce construction in a finished home but need service access and eventual replacement. Wired-power models need a planned power path. Separately, controls may use a wired or wireless communication link. The opening, product family, desired hardware and construction stage decide the better fit.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do shades integrate with Lutron lighting scenes, or run independently?</summary>
        <p>Compatible shades and lighting can share a programmed Lutron scene and supported wall control — for example, lower a shade and dim a room for viewing. DAVG checks the selected platform, shade and keypad combination, preserves a local override, and explains what a schedule will do.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is the largest Lutron shade I can use in my home?</summary>
        <p>There is no single maximum for every product. Published examples range from Triathlon Select up to 108 × 120 inches to larger specified Palladiom and Sivoia configurations. The fabric, width and drop pairing, area, seam, drive, bracket and mounting condition all have to pass the current model-specific sizing tool.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can a solar screen provide privacy at night?</summary>
        <p>Do not assume so. Openness, lighting on both sides of the glass and viewing angle change what is visible. Test a fabric at the real window and consider a translucent or blackout layer if evening privacy is required.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

